 @eazen  
Also, comparisons to other times, is exactly how we often figure out how much something is worth.   When it comes to video cards like this, using Nvidia as a example, they had a 2060 a 2070 and a 2080 video card, then they started coming out with super versions of those cards.  But in that case they have a 60 then a 60s card, then a 70 followed by 70s card.   Each card filled a slot in there line up.  Nvidia has always priced its video cards way too high, but understanding how their line up works requires us to look back at what they did previously.  Naming schemes makes it easier to figure out where a product will fall on that company's line up.   This card by name, is in fact at best at the upper end of there low end video cards. 
Oh, and lets not forget how manufacturing actually works in reality, I don't know if you have worked in a factory for any amount of time, but there is one rule that is always true.  It's simple, make it faster and make it better.  As time passes, not just more machines are used, but more efficient machines are used.  The processes that include actual people, are constantly being made to be more and more efficient.  The outcome of that is, it is always cheaper to make the new, than it was the make the older.   So no, it is not just so much more expensive to make a 7nm chip, that they have to charge us more for it.

